  "notes": "Pass 3 on the same estate of 115,535 entities, scored on CPU, with a fresh baseline whose noise floor is 2.4 %.\n\n- **A rule writes its links in one statement each way.** It used to insert and delete one row per statement. The target index is also built without copying a growing list for every target. Linking after the first push fell from 11 s to 1.5 s.\n- **A neighbour is derived only when its own derived values are read.** To count a repository's services, a cold build used to derive every service, then everything each service reads: workloads, Argo apps, deployments, cluster workloads. Now a path read only for a count or a stored value stops at what is stored. Status paths and paths that read a derived value still go all the way. A cold repository grid at 38,515 entities fell from 2.6 s to 0.17 s.\n\nTried and not kept:\n\n- Cheaper derivation per entity (narrow relation reads, one source lookup per pass, no list copies): inside the noise at this size.\n- Keeping entities in title order with one shared collator: 1.9 %, inside the noise. A sort is cheap next to the build it follows.\n\nFound along the way: the webhook test failed about one run in eight, because a local tool probed the fake webhook's port with a GET and the fake read every request as JSON. The fake now turns away anything but a POST. That fix is its own commit.\n\nThe final run was taken with the machine's load average at 36, which inflates CPU time too: it measured 2 % better than the baseline, where the kept attempts measured 9 %. Step by step the gains held: linking after the first push 10.9 s to 1.9 s, cold grids 13.9 s to 11.9 s.\n"
}
